# Answer 9 · "Language Is Gendered" — What It Implies, with Examples (CSS 2020)
@py question 2020-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Say what "gendered" implies (reflects, reproduces, can change), prove it with forms and examples, then the effects and remedies.
@ask ① WHAT DOES "language is gendered" IMPLY?
@ask ② EXPLAIN WITH EXAMPLES
(1) Introduction: language and power; thesis
(2) What it implies: reflects; reproduces; can change
(3) Theory: Sapir–Whorf; Spender 1980; Lakoff 1975
(4) Generic masculine: mankind; chairman; Urdu plurals
(5) Marked female terms: lady doctor; actress
(6) Semantic derogation: master/mistress; Schulz 1975
(7) Insults and idioms: choorian pehen lo; zan, zar, zameen
(8) Naming and address: Mrs; ghar wali
(9) Violence in words: "honour"; victim-blaming
(10) Conversation: hedges; interruptions; Tannen
(11) Effects: invisibility; stereotype; silence
(12) Change: inclusive terms; new words; Pakistani law
(13) Conclusion
